nativeloader: let an app on a system partition load its own libraries from class loaders it makes.

A class loader that an app creates itself (not through ApplicationLoaders) gets an isolated
namespace that may load anything under /data and /mnt/expand, so that apps with custom class
loaders can load their own libraries by absolute path (b/27588281). An app on a read-only system
partition keeps its libraries in its folder there instead. Chrome makes the class loaders of its
isolated splits itself; integrated into the image (/product/app/ChromePlay) it crashed at start:
"library .../ChromePlay.apk!/lib/x86_64/libelements.so ... is not accessible for the namespace"
(permitted_paths="/data:/mnt/expand"), while its base loaded libchrome.so from the same APK. The
namespace of such a class loader now also permits the folder of the calling APK when that is an
app folder on a system partition - the app's own files and nothing else.

diff --git a/libnativeloader/native_loader.cpp b/libnativeloader/native_loader.cpp
--- a/libnativeloader/native_loader.cpp
+++ b/libnativeloader/native_loader.cpp
@@ -171,6 +171,20 @@ Result<void*> TryLoadNativeloaderExtraLib(const char* path) {
         res.ok() ? "ok" : res.error().message().c_str());
   return res;
 }
+
+// The folder of an app on a read-only system partition ("/product/app/Chrome") when
+// `location` is one of the app's APKs.
+std::optional<std::string> SystemAppDir(const char* location) {
+  if (location == nullptr) {
+    return std::nullopt;
+  }
+  static const std::regex kAppApk("^(/(system|system_ext|product)/(app|priv-app)/[^/]+)/[^/]+\\.apk$");
+  std::cmatch match;
+  if (!std::regex_match(location, match, kAppApk)) {
+    return std::nullopt;
+  }
+  return match[1].str();
+}

